      "prologue": "Across the Tudor countryside, the roads were stalked by desperate families in rags. A catastrophic surge in population, recurring harvest failures, and soaring inflation pushed thousands of landless labourers to the edge of starvation. Meanwhile, ambitious landlords enclosed open fields to graze profitable sheep, tearing down cottages and driving peasants into the towns. Terrified of vagabond riots and crime, Elizabethan society underwent a profound revolution in thinking: transitioning from medieval charity and savage branding irons to England's first nationwide system of state-enforced welfare. Why did poverty and vagrancy escalate so rapidly in Elizabethan England, and how did the government's approach shift from punishment to state welfare?",
      "specification_anchor": "Elizabethan society in the Age of Exploration, 1558–88: the problem of the poor; causes of poverty and vagabondage (population growth, enclosure, sheep farming, bad harvests), distinguishing the deserving and idle poor, and the Poor Laws.",
      "hook_text": "Across the Tudor countryside, the roads were stalked by desperate families in rags. A catastrophic surge in population, recurring harvest failures, and soaring inflation pushed thousands of landless labourers to the edge of starvation. Meanwhile, ambitious landlords enclosed open fields to graze profitable sheep, tearing down cottages and driving peasants into the towns. Terrified of vagabond riots and crime, Elizabethan society underwent a profound revolution in thinking: transitioning from medieval charity and savage branding irons to England's first nationwide system of state-enforced welfare. Why did poverty and vagrancy escalate so rapidly in Elizabethan England, and how did the government's approach shift from punishment to state welfare?",
      "flashcards": [
        {
          "term": "Enclosure",
          "definition": "The practice of fencing off common arable fields into individual private pastures for sheep grazing, displacing agricultural labourers and driving rural depopulation.",
          "q": "What was enclosure, and why did it increase poverty?",
          "a": "Fencing off open common arable land for sheep farming, which required fewer workers and evicted tenant farmers into homelessness."
        },
        {
          "term": "Impotent Poor",
          "definition": "Impoverished individuals physically incapable of working due to age, illness, or disability, viewed by Elizabethans as deserving of Christian charity and relief.",
          "q": "Who were classified as the 'impotent poor'?",
          "a": "Those unable to work through no fault of their own, such as orphans, the elderly, sick, and physically disabled."
        },
        {
          "term": "Able-Bodied Poor",
          "definition": "Impoverished people who were physically capable of working but could not find employment due to harvest failures, trade slumps, or demographic growth.",
          "q": "Who were the 'able-bodied poor'?",
          "a": "Destitute citizens capable of labour who were unemployed due to economic conditions and were expected to work in exchange for assistance."
        },
        {
          "term": "Vagabond / Sturdy Beggar",
          "definition": "An itinerant, unemployed person who wandered seeking work or charity, viewed by authorities as a dangerous threat to social order and subject to whipping and imprisonment.",
          "q": "How did Elizabethan authorities view 'vagabonds'?",
          "a": "As idle, dishonest criminals and a threat to public order, subjecting them to public whipping, ear-boring, and execution for repeat offenses."
        },
        {
          "term": "Poor Rate",
          "definition": "A compulsory local property tax levied on parish householders by the Justice of the Peace to fund financial relief and work materials for the destitute.",
          "q": "What was the Elizabethan Poor Rate?",
          "a": "A compulsory tax collected in each parish to pay for relief and work materials for the local poor."
        },
        {
          "term": "1601 Act for the Relief of the Poor",
          "definition": "The culminating Poor Law legislation creating a unified national statutory framework administered by parish Overseers of the Poor, lasting until 1834.",
          "q": "What was the significance of the 1601 Elizabethan Poor Law?",
          "a": "It established a permanent, nationwide legal system dividing the poor into three classes and requiring parishes to provide work or relief."
        }
      ],
