      "prologue": "At the dawn of Elizabeth's reign, England was a small, insular nation whose sailors rarely dared venture beyond the English Channel or the cold waters of Iceland. Yet within three decades, English navigators shattered Spanish and Portuguese oceanic monopolies, braving frozen Arctic ice floes and rounding the uncharted, storm-tossed tip of Cape Horn. Driven by commercial desperation, advanced nautical science, and the lure of unmapped trade routes, Elizabethan mariners transformed England into a global seafaring superpower. How did navigational breakthroughs and commercial rivalry drive English voyages of exploration and Drake's circumnavigation of the globe?",
