      "prologue": "In the spring of 1584, Queen Elizabeth granted her ambitious court favourite Sir Walter Raleigh an extraordinary royal charter: permission to discover, conquer, and colonise any remote lands in North America not held by Christian princes. Raleigh envisioned a bustling New World empire named 'Virginia' in honour of the Virgin Queen, designed to challenge Spanish power and enrich the Crown. Instead, two disastrous expeditions to Roanoke Island were wrecked by fierce Atlantic shoals, starvation, and deadly conflict with local Secotan tribes—culminating in the chilling, unsolved disappearance of the 'Lost Colony'. Why did Walter Raleigh's attempts to establish a permanent English colony at Roanoke Island end in catastrophic failure?",
      "specification_anchor": "Elizabethan society in the Age of Exploration, 1558–88: Raleigh and the colonisation of Virginia; economic and strategic motivations for American colonisation, the 1585 and 1587 Roanoke expeditions, and reasons for the failure of the first English colony.",
      "hook_text": "In the spring of 1584, Queen Elizabeth granted her ambitious court favourite Sir Walter Raleigh an extraordinary royal charter: permission to discover, conquer, and colonise any remote lands in North America not held by Christian princes. Raleigh envisioned a bustling New World empire named 'Virginia' in honour of the Virgin Queen, designed to challenge Spanish power and enrich the Crown. Instead, two disastrous expeditions to Roanoke Island were wrecked by fierce Atlantic shoals, starvation, and deadly conflict with local Secotan tribes—culminating in the chilling, unsolved disappearance of the 'Lost Colony'. Why did Walter Raleigh's attempts to establish a permanent English colony at Roanoke Island end in catastrophic failure?",
